  <h2>Notice and repair the overstated science language</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="made" data-hint="Use the established research collocation for performing an experiment." data-fix="The laboratory conducted a controlled experiment last year." data-why="Conduct or carry out collocates with experiment; make an experiment is not the standard target phrase." data-success-message="Correct. The research action now uses the natural conduct-an-experiment collocation."><h3>Repair 1: experiment collocation</h3><p>Tap the verb that does not fit the research noun.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="laboratory">The laboratory</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="made">made</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="experiment">a controlled experiment</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="time">last year.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="accurate" data-hint="The readings agree with one another but all miss the reference in the same direction." data-fix="The readings are precise but biased: they cluster closely, yet all are two degrees above the reference." data-why="Clustering supports precision. Closeness to the reference concerns accuracy, and the systematic offset is evidence of bias." data-success-message="Correct. The repair distinguishes agreement among readings from agreement with the reference."><h3>Repair 2: accuracy versus precision</h3><p>Tap the measurement label the evidence does not support.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="readings">The sensor readings are</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="accurate">accurate</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="cluster">because they cluster closely,</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="reference">although all are two degrees above the reference.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="replicated" data-hint="Expert critique and a new-data study are different processes." data-fix="The article was peer-reviewed; an independent new-data study would be needed to test replication." data-why="Peer reviewers evaluate a manuscript. Replication requires another study using new evidence under the definition used in this lesson." data-success-message="Correct. The revision separates manuscript review from independent new-data replication."><h3>Repair 3: peer review versus replication</h3><p>Tap the scientific process that has been claimed without evidence.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="article">The article was</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="replicated">replicated</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="reviewers">when two reviewers commented on its method.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="ready" data-hint="A working version has not yet supplied seasonal, equipment, maintenance, or full-cost evidence." data-fix="The successful prototype supports a longer field evaluation; it is not yet validated for universal deployment." data-why="A prototype can demonstrate feasibility while scalability, reliability, interoperability, lifecycle cost, and real-world performance remain unresolved." data-success-message="Correct. The claim now preserves prototype promise without skipping the evidence needed for deployment."><h3>Repair 4: prototype versus readiness</h3><p>Tap the deployment claim the current stage cannot support.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="prototype">The prototype worked in the short spring pilot,</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="so">so the controller is</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="ready">ready for universal deployment.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p></div>

  <h2>State the evidence relationship before you reveal it</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-transform><p class="note-line">Respond aloud first. Name the question, design, finding, uncertainty, readiness stage, affected system, and next decision. Then reveal and compare.</p><div class="transform-list">
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>1</b> Report the energy result without adding statistical significance or universal scope.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The matched field trial found an observed 8.5% energy difference under the reported spring conditions; no uncertainty analysis was reported.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>2</b> Distinguish accurate measurement from precise repetition.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Accuracy concerns closeness to an accepted reference, while precision concerns agreement among repeated measurements.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>3</b> Distinguish computational reproducibility from a new-data replication.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Reproducibility uses the original data and analysis steps in this lesson; replication tests the question in a new study with new data.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>4</b> Give the prototype a promising but bounded readiness label.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The prototype is promising in the short spring pilot, but year-round performance, other equipment, full costs, and maintenance still need evidence.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>5</b> State the research relationship tested in the B2 listening assessment.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The pilot observed fewer reported delays, but the excluded busiest team and a simultaneous shift change mean a larger comparison is needed before attributing the improvement to the tool.</strong></p></div>
  </div></div>

  <h2>Practice science communication without exposing private or proprietary information</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>You never need to discuss a real diagnosis, treatment choice, disability, confidential dataset, unpublished invention, employer security system, trade secret, patent dispute, financial investment, product failure, laboratory incident, or personal belief about a disputed scientific issue. Use Marlowe or a tutor-created source. The skill is accurate evidence and readiness language, not disclosure or ideological agreement.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Claim ladder:</b> Move a fictional result through <span class="ex">observed, suggests, supports, demonstrates under these conditions</span>, and <span class="ex">does not establish</span>. Defend each strength from the source.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Measurement switch:</b> Make repeated readings more precise but less accurate, then recalibrate the instrument. Reformulate every measurement claim.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Publication switch:</b> Change a press release into a labeled preprint, then add peer review, released code, and an independent new-data study. State what each stage adds and does not add.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Readiness switch:</b> Move an idea from proof of concept to prototype, pilot, deployment, and scale-up. Name the new evidence and support required at each stage.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Risk switch:</b> Change a hazard's likelihood, consequence, detectability, fallback response, or affected group. Revise the risk statement and safeguard.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Final production: deliver a two-minute responsible innovation review</h3><p>Use Marlowe or a new fictional technology. Identify the problem, research question, hypothesis or other inquiry type, prediction if relevant, method, sample, comparison, variables, primary outcome, numerical finding, uncertainty, source, publication status, and strongest defensible interpretation. Distinguish accuracy, precision, reliability, reproducibility or replication, and generalizability wherever the source allows.</p><p>Name the current readiness stage and decision authority. Evaluate feasibility, interoperability, maintenance, lifecycle cost, accessibility, one hazard, the associated risk under a stated framework, one expected benefit, one trade-off, and one unintended consequence. Recommend further research, a limited pilot, wider deployment, revision, or rejection, then state the monitoring and stop conditions. Use eight science or innovation collocations, three word families with accurate stress, and one promising-not-proven thought-group correction. Your listener changes the season, sample, failure rate, comparison, or cost; revise immediately.</p><p><b>Listener check:</b> Can the listener identify what was asked, what was measured, what was found, which uncertainty remains, what stage the technology has reached, who may decide, who maintains it, what could go wrong, and what evidence would justify the next stage?</p></div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You preserved scientific design, measurement meaning, publication status, readiness, risk, evidence scope, and the B2 research-assessment relationship."><h3>Seven final decisions</h3><div class="quiz-list">
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The matched randomized field design reduces confounding, and the observed 8.5% difference is compatible with a controller effect in the studied rooms; unreported uncertainty and narrow conditions limit the wider claim." data-hint="Preserve design strength, observed difference, studied scope, unreported uncertainty, and generalizability limits." data-fix="The design strengthens a bounded causal interpretation, while the unreported uncertainty prevents a stronger conclusion."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which interpretation best matches the Marlowe design and limits?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The matched randomized field design reduces confounding, and the observed 8.5% difference is compatible with a controller effect in the studied rooms; unreported uncertainty and narrow conditions limit the wider claim.">The matched randomized field design reduces confounding, and the observed 8.5% difference is compatible with a controller effect in the studied rooms; unreported uncertainty and narrow conditions limit the wider claim.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The short pilot proves year-round performance in every warehouse and climate.">The short pilot proves year-round performance in every warehouse and climate.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Random assignment means the observed measurements can be ignored.">Random assignment means the observed measurements can be ignored.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Closely grouped readings can be precise but inaccurate if they share a systematic offset from the accepted reference." data-hint="Keep repeated agreement separate from closeness to the reference." data-fix="Precision can coexist with a systematic accuracy problem."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which statement distinguishes precision from accuracy?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Closely grouped readings can be precise but inaccurate if they share a systematic offset from the accepted reference.">Closely grouped readings can be precise but inaccurate if they share a systematic offset from the accepted reference.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Precise always means perfectly accurate in every context.">Precise always means perfectly accurate in every context.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Accuracy means that repeated values are far apart.">Accuracy means that repeated values are far apart.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Peer review provides expert critique; replication requires a new study with new data under the definition used here." data-hint="Separate manuscript evaluation from independent new evidence." data-fix="Reviewers critique a report; a new study tests replication."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which sentence handles peer review and replication accurately?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Peer review provides expert critique; replication requires a new study with new data under the definition used here.">Peer review provides expert critique; replication requires a new study with new data under the definition used here.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Two editorial comments automatically replicate every finding.">Two editorial comments automatically replicate every finding.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A preprint is false by definition because it is public.">A preprint is false by definition because it is public.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The working prototype has field-pilot evidence, but wider deployment still needs seasonal, equipment, fault, maintenance, and full-cost evidence." data-hint="Name the achieved stage and the missing readiness evidence." data-fix="The prototype has limited field evidence, not complete deployment evidence."><p class="quiz-question">4. Which readiness statement is calibrated?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The working prototype has field-pilot evidence, but wider deployment still needs seasonal, equipment, fault, maintenance, and full-cost evidence.">The working prototype has field-pilot evidence, but wider deployment still needs seasonal, equipment, fault, maintenance, and full-cost evidence.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A proof of concept and global deployment are always the same stage.">A proof of concept and global deployment are always the same stage.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A new product needs no maintenance or operational support.">A new product needs no maintenance or operational support.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The sensor fault is a hazard; the risk assessment considers its likelihood, consequence, detection, fallback behavior, and affected operation under the stated framework." data-hint="Separate the potential source of harm from the structured estimate around it." data-fix="The failed sensor is the hazard; its likelihood and consequences inform risk."><p class="quiz-question">5. Which statement separates hazard from risk?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The sensor fault is a hazard; the risk assessment considers its likelihood, consequence, detection, fallback behavior, and affected operation under the stated framework.">The sensor fault is a hazard; the risk assessment considers its likelihood, consequence, detection, fallback behavior, and affected operation under the stated framework.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Hazard and risk always have one identical universal score.">Hazard and risk always have one identical universal score.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A possible benefit removes every hazard automatically.">A possible benefit removes every hazard automatically.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The observed energy difference is promising, but full cost-effectiveness requires a comparator, time horizon, outcomes, lifecycle costs, and uncertainty." data-hint="Do not turn three listed prices into a complete economic evaluation." data-fix="The source supports a promising energy result, not a complete cost-effectiveness conclusion."><p class="quiz-question">6. Which sentence reports value without overstating economic evidence?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The observed energy difference is promising, but full cost-effectiveness requires a comparator, time horizon, outcomes, lifecycle costs, and uncertainty.">The observed energy difference is promising, but full cost-effectiveness requires a comparator, time horizon, outcomes, lifecycle costs, and uncertainty.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The $1,800 hardware price proves permanent profit in every market.">The $1,800 hardware price proves permanent profit in every market.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Cost-effectiveness means that a product has any listed price.">Cost-effectiveness means that a product has any listed price.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The pilot reduced reported delays, but a larger comparison is needed before claiming the tool caused the improvement." data-hint="Match the B2 listening assessment's observed result, excluded team, simultaneous change, and next comparison." data-fix="Report fewer delays while preserving the need for a larger causal comparison."><p class="quiz-question">7. Which summary matches the B2 research-briefing assessment?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The pilot reduced reported delays, but a larger comparison is needed before claiming the tool caused the improvement.">The pilot reduced reported delays, but a larger comparison is needed before claiming the tool caused the improvement.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The scheduling tool certainly caused every improvement in every team.">The scheduling tool certainly caused every improvement in every team.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The pilot reported no change in delays and no competing explanation.">The pilot reported no change in delays and no competing explanation.</button></div></div>
  </div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p></div>

  <h2>Reflect: can the evidence still be seen through the innovation claim?</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Explain the complete evidence-to-adoption chain</h3><p>Complete these lines aloud: <span class="ex">The question is...</span> <span class="ex">The design compares...</span> <span class="ex">The primary outcome is...</span> <span class="ex">The finding is...</span> <span class="ex">The uncertainty is...</span> <span class="ex">The publication status is...</span> <span class="ex">The current readiness stage is...</span> <span class="ex">The hazard is...</span> <span class="ex">The risk depends on...</span> <span class="ex">The next evidence should...</span></p><p>Then change the sample, assignment, reference value, publication status, equipment, season, failure rate, cost, or decision authority. Reformulate every claim and recommendation that no longer fits.</p><p><a href="#next-day-retrieval">Return tomorrow for the five-item retrieval check</a> before rereading the lesson.</p></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Next use:</b> Choose one nonpersonal science or technology report. Write three sentences: what the source observed, what it does not yet establish, and which evidence would justify the next stage.</div>
  <section id="next-day-retrieval" data-lesson-extension="Next-day hypothesis-prediction, accuracy-precision, publication-replication, readiness-stage, and B2 research-claim retrieval"><h2>Next-day retrieval</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div><p>Return without rereading. Reconstruct the scientific question, measurement meaning, publication status, readiness boundary, and assessment-aligned claim in five new fictional contexts.</p>
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You retained hypothesis-prediction, accuracy-precision, peer-review-replication, prototype-deployment, and evidence-calibrated assessment language."><h3>Five delayed decisions</h3><div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The hypothesis proposes that the coating reduces heat transfer; the prediction is that coated panels will show a smaller temperature change in the stated test." data-hint="Separate the proposed explanatory relationship from its observable test result." data-fix="The hypothesis proposes the relationship; the prediction states the expected measurement."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which sentence distinguishes a hypothesis from a prediction?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The hypothesis proposes that the coating reduces heat transfer; the prediction is that coated panels will show a smaller temperature change in the stated test.">The hypothesis proposes that the coating reduces heat transfer; the prediction is that coated panels will show a smaller temperature change in the stated test.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A hypothesis is a proven result and a prediction is a completed deployment.">A hypothesis is a proven result and a prediction is a completed deployment.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The two words mean that no measurement is needed.">The two words mean that no measurement is needed.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The readings are precise because they cluster closely, but their shared offset from the reference raises an accuracy problem." data-hint="Use repeated agreement for one label and reference agreement for the other." data-fix="The cluster is precise, while the offset challenges accuracy."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which sentence interprets the measurement pattern accurately?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The readings are precise because they cluster closely, but their shared offset from the reference raises an accuracy problem.">The readings are precise because they cluster closely, but their shared offset from the reference raises an accuracy problem.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A shared offset proves perfect accuracy.">A shared offset proves perfect accuracy.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Closely grouped readings are imprecise by definition.">Closely grouped readings are imprecise by definition.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Peer review critiques the report; a new independent study with new data tests replication under this lesson's definition." data-hint="Keep expert manuscript evaluation separate from a new evidence-producing study." data-fix="Peer review is critique, while replication here requires new data."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which sentence preserves the two scientific processes?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Peer review critiques the report; a new independent study with new data tests replication under this lesson's definition.">Peer review critiques the report; a new independent study with new data tests replication under this lesson's definition.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A preprint has already been replicated because it has a web address.">A preprint has already been replicated because it has a web address.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Replication means correcting the article's punctuation.">Replication means correcting the article's punctuation.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The prototype supports a limited pilot, but deployment still requires field reliability, maintenance, interoperability, cost, and risk evidence." data-hint="Preserve the achieved working-version stage and name the missing operational evidence." data-fix="The prototype justifies the next test, not automatic deployment."><p class="quiz-question">4. Which recommendation respects the readiness stage?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The prototype supports a limited pilot, but deployment still requires field reliability, maintenance, interoperability, cost, and risk evidence.">The prototype supports a limited pilot, but deployment still requires field reliability, maintenance, interoperability, cost, and risk evidence.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="One successful demonstration proves permanent global scale-up.">One successful demonstration proves permanent global scale-up.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A working prototype means maintenance is impossible.">A working prototype means maintenance is impossible.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The six-week pilot observed fewer reported delays, but an excluded team and a simultaneous shift change justify a larger comparison before a causal claim." data-hint="Preserve the observed outcome, two competing explanations, and proportionate next evidence." data-fix="The pilot result is promising, while the design limits require a larger causal comparison."><p class="quiz-question">5. Which delayed summary matches the B2 research briefing?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The six-week pilot observed fewer reported delays, but an excluded team and a simultaneous shift change justify a larger comparison before a causal claim.">The six-week pilot observed fewer reported delays, but an excluded team and a simultaneous shift change justify a larger comparison before a causal claim.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The tool certainly caused every change because the pilot lasted six weeks.">The tool certainly caused every change because the pilot lasted six weeks.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The briefing contains no result, limitation, or proposed comparison.">The briefing contains no result, limitation, or proposed comparison.</button></div></div>
